   "source": [
    "**Exercise 3.2**\n",
    "\n",
    "The columns num_Genres and num_MoodsAll are pratically empty, becoming unusable\n",
    "\n",
    "THe columns AllMusic Extraction Date and Sample show nearly constant results, with AllMusic Extraction being the date of download and Sample being a binary flag indicating the existance of a sample.\n",
    "\n",
    "The Relevance column represents the score returned by AllMusic for the query that retrieved the track, so doesn't measure any intrinsic musical measuserement. Similarly the Sample column is merely a binary flag rather than the audio data itself\n",
    "\n",
    "The Duration column is labelled in seconds but mixes units: besides normal values in seconds, some rows store it in milliseconds (e.g. 367000) or in minutes (e.g. 3.5), so it measures something other than what its name states."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "384d59c2",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Exercise 3.5**\n",
    "\n",
    "In the training set, there are 625 unique artists (655 before normalizing artist-name spellings). The number of songs per artist ranges from 1 up to a maximum of 17 (Billie Holiday), with an average of 1.49 songs per artist.\n",
    "\n",
    "Crucially, when comparing the partitions, 223 songs in the test set share an artist with the training data. This means that approximately 54% of the test partition (223 out of 409 songs) is not truly independent from the training set in terms of authorship. In music emotion recognition, if a model learns an artist's specific acoustic signature or voice rather than general emotional features, its performance will be artificially inflated on these overlapping artists."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 5,
   "id": "d5ee82d2",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
